  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Consider three persons as one unit. This unit and remaining <math><mn>47</mn></math> persons (total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>48</mn></math>)&nbsp;can be arranged in a circle in <math><mn>47</mn><mo>!</mo><mi>&#160;</mi></math>&nbsp;ways.<br /><br />In this unit, the two brothers can be interchanged in <math><mn>2</mn></math> ways.<br /><br />The person between the two brothers can be any of the remaining <math><mn>48</mn></math> persons who can be selected in <math> <mrow> <mmultiscripts> <mi>C</mi> <mprescripts></mprescripts> <mo>&#160;</mo> <mrow> <mn>48</mn></mrow> </mmultiscripts> <msub> <mrow></mrow> <mn>1</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math> ways.<br /><br />Hence, the required number of ways <math> <mrow> <mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts> <mi>C</mi> <mprescripts></mprescripts> <mo>&#160;</mo> <mrow> <mn>48</mn></mrow> </mmultiscripts> <msub> <mrow></mrow> <mn>1</mn> </msub> <mo>&#215;</mo><mn>47</mn><mo>!</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>48</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow> </math>"
  }
